ur « As Columns ».\n   Toute capsule interactive doit être une vraie pilule `border-radius:999px`, jamais un rectangle vaguement arrondi.\n\n## Courbes et durées — règle exécutable\n\n- Courbe de décélération par défaut : **EaseOutExpo** — CSS `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` ; en JavaScript\n  `x === 1 ? 1 : 1 - 2 ** (-10 * x)`. Ne pas substituer le `smooth` natif du navigateur quand la durée ou\n  la courbe doit être précise.\n- `0,15 s` : micro-interactions fréquentes (boutons, hover, toggles). `0,10 s` est trop saccadé/arcade et\n  ne laisse pas percevoir l'animation.\n- `0,20–0,25 s` : durée standard polyvalente.\n- `0,30–0,35 s` : panneaux, modales, grands déplacements. Pour la navigation Finder par lettre : `0,30 s`.\n- Règle centrale : plus l'action est fréquente et courte, plus elle est rapide ; plus la surface ou le\n  déplacement est grand et important, plus la durée peut augmenter.\n- Respecter `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` : résultat immédiat, sans animation.\n\n## Écriture (après chaque composant construit ou feedback de design reçu)\n\n1. Appender en FIN de fichier (Partie III) une section datée `## YYYY-MM-DD — <sujet>` : ce qui a été\n   construit, le retour de Matthieu s'il y en a un, toute DIVERGENCE trouvée entre la doctrine (Parties I/II)\n   et le code vivant réellement vérifié (grep/lecture directe, jamais une supposition) — les divergences\n   valent autant que les confirmations, ne jamais les taire.\n2. Mettre à jour l'index : appender une ligne au tableau du dernier `INDEX du fichier`. S'il est devenu trop\n   faux, appender un nouvel `## INDEX du fichier` complet daté (jamais modifier l'ancien).\n3. Une divergence non tranchée reste OUVERTE jusqu'à ce que Matthieu décide — ne jamais choisir à sa place\n   silencieusement ; le composant construit entre-temps suit le CODE VIVANT (source exécutable), pas la\n   doctrine écrite si les deux divergent.\n4. Commit automatique (repo Memory, sans demander).\n5. Pour chaque nouvelle référence reçue, préciser dans l'append si elle est `proposition`, `norme retenue`,\n   `implémentée` et/ou `validée`. Si elle remplace une règle ancienne, écrire explicitement « remplace … » :\n   ne jamais laisser le lecteur futur deviner quelle version prévaut.\n\n## Ce que le fichier sait déjà (ne pas réinventer)\n\n- La leçon centrale (Partie I) : réussir un design = RETIRER jusqu'à la limpidité, pas ajouter. Une couleur\n  d'accent, graisses légères, toujours dire sur quoi un élément agit, ton README impersonnel décomplexifié.\n- Le système complet (Partie II) : tokens (`src/theme.js` : `PALETTE`, `THEMES`, `glassPanel`, `SHADOWS`,\n  `RADII`), le glass `.zr-liquid` (blur+saturate+liseré blanc inset, jamais un border plein), les 3 polices\n  Anthropic et leurs rôles stricts, le Finder macOS (vue Create), l'éditeur BlockNote, les composants\n  signature (MacWindow, QuickLook, Sidebar), les 8 règles transverses non négociables.\n- Divergences connues (Partie III, à vérifier avant de s'y fier) : accent corail (doctrine) vs bleu système\n  (code vivant) depuis au moins le 2026-07-14 — non tranché.\n\n## Construire un nouveau composant UI — check-list courte\n\n1. Lire l'index Design1.md (ci-dessus) + `src/theme.js` du projet concerné (source de vérité exécutable).\n2. Chercher un composant existant à imiter plutôt qu'inventer (`QuickLookOverlay.jsx` pour un overlay/portal,\n   `ClipboardProvider.jsx` pour un raccourci clavier global bien gardé contre le vol de focus).\n3. Construire, `node --check` ou `esbuild --bundle` pour la syntaxe, `vite build` pour une vérification\n   complète avant de toucher au navigateur.\n4. Vérifier en RÉEL dans le navigateur (dev local) — jamais annoncer un composant visuel fonctionnel sans\n   l'avoir vu tourner. Piège connu (2026-07-14) : un cache IndexedDB corrompu par des tests intensifs peut\n   casser le rendu de l'app SANS rapport avec le nouveau composant — avant de suspecter son propre code,\n   tester si un clic natif préexistant (ex. le fil d'Ariane) échoue PAREIL ; si oui, vider\n   `localStorage`/IndexedDB du site (`indexedDB.databases()` puis `deleteDatabase`) et recharger avant de\n   continuer à débugger.\n5. Appender l'entrée Design1.md (section Écriture ci-dessus) avant de considérer la tâche terminée.\n\n## Skills liés\n\n- `social-media` : patron dont ce skill est directement calqué (lecture indexée + écriture datée append-only).\n- `hardware-check` : parallélisme si le composant implique un traitement média lourd en parallèle du design.\n\n--- SKILL think ---\n---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass on any decision or change in any domain — code, architecture, product, business, life/admin, travel, purchases, health, learning, art/3D/creative. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), verifies the real facts instead of guessing from memory (grep the codebase, read the actual documents and numbers, search official sources), checks a 12-axis impact surface (existing state, conflicts, limits and deadlines, security/legal, partial failure, backward compatibility, scale, recurring cost, observability, edge cases, checks, reversibility), runs a domain-specific premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks with exact proof and a concrete verification each, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict at paid-specialist precision, never generic. Use whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change or decision might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, without the phrase, before shipping anything touching shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, a public API/route — or any decision that is expensive, irreversible, or deadline-bound.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nSurface the FEW risks that matter on ANY decision — code, business, life, art — each proven against the real facts, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. This pass answers the question asked, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against reality instead of memory.\n\n**Specialist rule (anti-generic), applies to every output line:** write at the level of the expert who gets paid to review exactly this — immigration lawyer, senior rigger, tax advisor, DB architect. Every kept risk must contain at least one exact value from the real context (number, date, name, path, price, spec) verified this session. A risk that could be pasted unchanged into someone else's situation is generic: sharpen it or cut it.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (two lines)\n\n- Domain + the specialist to embody + type: (a) action to take, (b) existing thing to audit, (c) pure decision, nothing built yet.\n- For (b), the source of truth is the actual artifact (`git diff`, the contract, the scene file), never its description.\n- Trivial, cheap and reversible case: say so, jump to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is for anything expensive, irreversible, deadline-bound, or touching shared state, money, auth, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions the specialist would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Verify the real facts (never from memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim cites its proof — file:line, URL of an official source, a measured value, or a posed calculation — found THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSources by domain:\n\n- **Code**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo — every call site, not just the edited file. Hyrum's law: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior (output format, ordering, timing, error text)? Does the new code re-read data written by the old code?\n- **Business / life / admin**: read the actual documents (contracts, statements, notes, Memory files); web-search official sources for external facts (rules, prices, deadlines, opening days); compute the real numbers and dates — count, never estimate.\n- **Physical / creative (3D, drawing, video, print)**: open the actual source files (scene, layers, footage); check the official specs (software docs, platform limits, physical medium constraints); measure, don't eyeball.\n- **People**: who else is affected, who must agree, whose workflow breaks if this changes?\n\nIf the facts can't be reached, label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 universal axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Existing state**: does what already exists (data, commitments, acquired rights) stay valid? Partial overwrite possible?\n2. **Conflicts / concurrency**: two actors or processes on the same resource at once — then what?\n3. **Limits & deadlines**: which cap (budget, size, rate, date, capacity) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security / legal**: who can trigger or abuse this? Compliant with law, terms, licenses?\n5. **Partial failure**: stopped halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, clients, habits, commitments still work?\n7. **Scale & durability**: still fine at 100x the volume, or repeated weekly for a year?\n8. **Recurring cost**: money, time, energy that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it fails silently, how and when do you find out?\n10. **Edge cases**: empty/first-run, oversized, duplicate, offline; holiday, sick day, exception case.\n11. **Checks**: a test or verification that catches the regression BEFORE real impact?\n12. **Reversibility**: one action to undo? Does what got engaged meanwhile (money sent, word given, data written) survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes, expressed in the domain's own units and vocabulary. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks later it failed, and it was THIS decision. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Use THIS domain's high-frequency classics — code: off-by-one, empty/first-run state, stale cache, non-idempotent retry, timezone/encoding, two concurrent writers; admin/life: missed deadline, missing document, misread eligibility rule, transfer delay, closed office; business: nobody pays, no distribution channel; 3D/art/print: wrong units or scale, topology breaks at rig, export color shift, asset license.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → irreversible loss) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (per Step 2) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it: a command, a test, a call, a calculation, a measurement.\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nDécision/changement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the facts changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- It audits ONE stated decision or change. It does not generate unsolicited master plans.\n- Not a substitute for tests, CI, or licensed professionals on high-stakes legal, medical or financial moves: it catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f671c-4526-7610-acc2-41e1641f1175"}}}
